<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="urgent-actions"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Do the urgent actions before administration</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Stop engaging with the suspicious contact. If money, card details or banking access may be involved, contact the bank or card provider through an official app, statement or independently located website. Ask what protective action is available and record the provider’s response and reference number.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If an account may be affected, use the provider’s official security or recovery route from a trusted device. If remote access occurred, tell the bank when financial information may have been visible. For reporting options, use <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://www.cyber.gov.au/report-and-recover/report">ReportCyber guidance</a>; call 000 for an immediate threat to life or risk of harm.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="timeline-and-observations"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Write a simple private timeline</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Record the date and approximate time, how contact arrived, what you saw, what you did and what happened next. Use broad categories such as “email address”, “password”, “card detail”, “verification code”, “identity document” or “remote access”. Do not write the actual secret, full number or code.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Separate <strong>observations</strong> from <strong>inferences</strong>. For example:</p><ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-muted"><li><strong>Observation:</strong> “At about 2:10 pm, a caller said they were from a delivery company and asked me to install remote-support software.”</li><li><strong>Inference:</strong> “This may have been a remote-access scam.”</li></ul><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">That distinction keeps the record accurate without claiming that an unfamiliar sign-in proves an attack or that no warning proves an account is safe.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="organise-and-report"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Keep useful copies without creating new risk</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Keep original emails, messages, invoices or screenshots where it is safe to do so. Make redacted working copies for sharing, removing passwords, verification codes, full payment numbers, identity-document numbers and unnecessary personal details. Do not reopen suspicious links or attachments to retrieve more material.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Use a private folder with a clear date-based name. Record the organisation contacted, the channel used, the time, the reference number and the next owner. A report reference shows that a report was made; it does not prove the report’s conclusion or guarantee investigation.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="next-owner-and-follow-up"><span aria-hidden="true">04</span>Track decisions and follow-up</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Make a short action list with one owner and status for each item: bank contacted, card reviewed, account recovery started, provider reference saved, report submitted, trusted contact told and follow-up date. Add only the information needed to complete the action.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Be alert to follow-up recovery scams. Do not pay an unexpected helper who promises guaranteed recovery or asks for remote access, passwords or codes. If you need support, choose the bank, provider, official reporting service or a trusted local person through independently verified details. The <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://www.scamwatch.gov.au/stop-check-protect/what-to-do-if-youve-been-scammed">Scamwatch guidance</a> includes support and reporting pathways, but no source can promise a particular bank or recovery outcome.</p>
